
<p>Apple Developer:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Starting today with the iOS 27.3, iPadOS 27.3, macOS 27.3, and
visionOS 27.3 betas, a new Developer Preview enables any developer
to integrate their app with Siri AI, giving businesses and
developers new ways to reach and serve their customers. [...]</p>

<p>This developer beta offers a sneak peek at Siri AI app intents for
developers. With this early Developer Preview, your app can work
directly with Siri AI, giving you a first look at new ways to
reach and serve your customers. This preview provides early access
before first shipping to customers in a few months; and with
submission to the App Store opening up in the spring, developers
have time to explore, build, and help shape their experiences
before launch. Try it out and share your feedback.</p>

<p>As you explore early, we will follow up with more details about
the terms and usage policies for experiences enabled through Siri
AI. More to share soon.</p>
</blockquote>

<p><a href="https://mastodon.social/@agiletortoise/117554110669725591">Greg Pierce</a> (developer of <a href="https://getdrafts.com/">Drafts</a> and <a href="https://agiletortoise.com/">other fine apps</a>):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>This sounds amazing, and I didn’t think we’d see App Intents
integration into Siri AI until next WWDC.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Siri AI is getting to the point of underpromising and overdelivering, which is where you want products to be. In 2025 things were <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2025/03/something_is_rotten_in_the_state_of_cupertino">so bad</a>, Apple had overpromised and not just underdelivered, but delivered nothing.</p>

